  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 334 AM EST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nEarly morning GOES satellite imagery reveals lake effect clouds \nacross the UP however radar returns have gone quiet. Incoming \nridging aloft and sfc high pressure will continue to lower lake \ninduced inversion heights below 5k ft, reducing the chance for \nanymore meaningful lake effect snow/rain showers through the rest of \nthe day. A weak embedded trough rotating around the eastern seaboard \nclosed low swings just east of the UP this morning, potentially \nreinforcing some lake LES showers to the central and eastern UP, \nhowever, with reduce inversion heights and bufkit soundings \nrevealing drier sub-cloud layers, thinking nothing more than a few \nisolated flurries will be seen. Otherwise, temps in the 30s to low \n40s are expected today, warmest south. Clearing skies and light \nwinds tonight may result in western and interior central UP temps \nfalling into the teens and 20s tonight, though this may be \ncontingent on how quickly we can rid lake effect clouds from west to \neast.\n\nHigh pressure dominates the forecast from Sunday into the first half \nof next week, and as such expect dry, mild weather to prevail \nthrough at least Wednesday. Temperatures increase back into the 40s \nfor most locations by Sunday, then reside well above seasonal norms \nwith highs in the upper 40s to mid 50s for much of next week. The \nnext low looks to lift out of the Plains in the Wednesday night-\nThursday time frame, but PoPs remain low for now as the bulk of the \nmedium range ensemble guidance depicts this system passing north \ninto Ontario. Guidance does hint at another system bringing more \nwidespread precipitation back to the UP towards next weekend, but \nconfidence in any specific details remains low at this time.",
